   211	
   212	
   213	
   214	to the entire ecosystem. Although RUF are widely used by Rust
   215	developers, unfortunately, to the best of our knowledge, its usage
   216	and impacts on the whole Rust ecosystem have not been studied so
   217	far.
   218	
   219	To fill this gap, this paper conducts the first in-depth study to
   220	analyze RUF usage and its impacts on the whole Rust ecosystem.
   221	We begin by extracting RUF definitions from the compiler and their
   222	usage from packages. Following that, we resolve all dependencies
   223	across the entire ecosystem, allowing us to quantify RUF impacts on
   224	an ecosystem scale. Though conceptually simple, we must resolve
   225	three challenges to achieve the analysis.
   226	First, it is hard to extract RUF. There is no official documentation
   227	to specify RUF definitions and usage, and they frequently change
   228	with compiler release updates. As a result, the syntax of RUF is
   229	not unified, and no existing technique can extract RUF accurately.
   230	To resolve this challenge, we develop new techniques to track all
   231	RUF supported by each version of the Rust compiler and all RUF
   232	used by Rust packages in the ecosystem. Second, the Rust package
   233	manager cannot be used to analyze the entire ecosystem in an
   234	acceptable time. Existing dependency-resolving techniques [ 17,
   235	39, 66 ] fail to cover all dependency types and use approximate
   236	resolution algorithms, leading to inaccurate dependency resolution.
   237	Therefore, we propose an accurate Ecosystem Dependency Graph
   238	(EDG) generator to resolve dependencies in the Rust ecosystem.
   239	Third, it is challenging to quantify RUF impacts precisely. RUF
   240	impacts other packages conditionally, determined by both RUF
   241	configuration and dependency attributes. Therefore, we propose the
   242	semantic identification of RUF configuration to precisely identify
   243	RUF usage and convey its impacts.
   244	By conquering the above challenges, we analyze all packages on
   245	the official package database crates.io and resolve 592,183 package
   246	versions to get 139,525,225 transitive dependencies and 182,026
   247	RUF configurations. Our highlighted findings are: 1) About half
   248	of RUF (47%) are not stabilized in the latest version of the Rust
   249	compiler; 2) 72,132 (12%) package versions in the Rust ecosystem
   250	are using RUF, and 90% of package versions among them are still
   251	using unstabilized RUF; 3) Through dependency propagation, RUF
   252	can impact at most 259,540 (44%) package versions, causing at most
   253	70,913 (12%) versions to suffer from compilation failure. To further
   254	mitigate RUF impacts, we propose a new technique to detect RUF
   255	
   256	
   257	
   258	impacts on packages and recover them if RUF make them suffer
   259	from compilation failure. Theoretically, 90% compilation failure
   260	caused by RUF impacts can be recovered. With our novel techniques,
   261	our work further advances the state of the art in analyzing RUF
   262	usage and impacts. Our unique findings reveal the stability problems
   263	of the Rust compiler, and based on these insights, we offer practical
   264	mitigation tools and suggestions to foster a more reliable Rust
   265	ecosystem.
   266	The contributions of this paper can be summarized as follows:
   267	
   268	
   269	- **New Study.** We are the first to investigate the usage and impacts
   270	of RUF in the Rust ecosystem. Our study demystifies RUF from
   271	three aspects: How RUF evolve with compiler upgrades, how
   272	Rust packages use RUF, and how RUF impact packages through
   273	dependencies.
   274	
   275	- **New Technique.** We propose novel techniques to extract RUF
   276	and determine RUF impacts in the Rust ecosystem. First, we propose the _compilation data-flow interception_ to extract RUF usage
   277	of Rust packages precisely. Second, we propose _semantic identifi-_
   278	_cation of RUF configuration_ to recognize the semantics among RUF
   279	configurations. Third, we propose _ecosystem dependency graph_
   280	_generator_ to efficiently and accurately determine RUF impacts
   281	through dependencies. Last, we propose _RUF impact mitigation_
   282	to help recover from compilation failure.
   283	
   284	- **Ecosystem-scale Analysis.** We conduct the first ecosystemscale analysis with 140 million transitive dependencies counted
   285	to reveal the RUF impacts. In our analysis, we find that RUF can
   286	impact almost half (44%) of the Rust ecosystem, revealing the
   287	significant impacts and corresponding problems of RUF in the
   288	Rust ecosystem.
   289	
   290	- **Community Contributions.** We design and implement a RUFcompilation-failure recovery tool that can recover up to 90%
   291	of RUF compilation failures. We open source all RUF analysis
   292	implementations, tools, and data sets to the public to help the
   293	community track and fix RUF problems [1] .
   294	
